          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies differ about delayed prescribing for respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "Only partly. The results don't clearly show a disagreement about delayed prescribing for respiratory infections.\n\nThe upper respiratory infection guidance (*Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated*) says that for colds, most sore throats, acute bronchitis, and early uncomplicated sinusitis, not prescribing antibiotics is the active recommendation. It says these infections are mostly viral and that antibiotics do not shorten them. This document has a section on disagreements, but the passage returned to me did not include it, so I can't tell you what it says.\n\nThe only disagreement in the results is about urinary tract infections, not breathing-related infections. The urinary tract guidance (*Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence*) says some European guidance supports offering symptom treatment with delayed or no antibiotics for mild bladder infections, while other guidance keeps immediate antibiotics as the default. Per that document, the disagreement is about how much antibiotic exposure is worth avoiding, not about the facts.\n\nTo go further, you would need the disagreement section of the upper respiratory infection guidance. A clinician can also tell you how these general positions apply to a specific situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
